+
+
+## Experiment-controlled recording in the native window
+
+An integrator can launch the ordinary GUI with `--control-dir <absolute-private-directory> --control-instance <32-hex-token>`. This opts in to a local filesystem mailbox; it does not start recording or create another tracking engine. `endpoint.json` identifies the instance, backend, process and supported capabilities. Requests and replies use matching random IDs, an instance token and bounded expiry.
+
+`record_start` accepts a fresh absolute output `directory`, with `raw` and `debug` options. A reply means the native worker received the command; recording is established only when status names that fresh take and at least one video frame has been accepted. `record_stop` requires that take's `expected_directory` and an immutable `stop_request_id`; it cannot stop another take. Wait for that directory's `completion.json`, successful writer drain and matching accepted/written counts. Its `control_stop_id` proves the capture worker stopped the owned take. An earlier manual stop has no such proof; `capture_pause_count` also records intervening capture pauses. `pause` pauses tracking, while Stop only ends recording. The native window and source can remain running for another take.
